.TH V4-DUMPINFO 1 "October 28, 2026" "unix4-toolchain" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
v4-dumpinfo \- inspect v4linux crash dumps
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B v4-dumpinfo
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-b ]
.RB [ \-y ]
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-s\ \fIwords\fR ]
.RB [ \-m\ \fIaddr\fR[\fB,\fIlen\fR] ]
.RB [ \-x\ \fIformat\fR
.BI \-o\  file ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.I dumpfile
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I v4-dumpinfo
inspects crash dumps generated by the \fBv4linux\fR emulator
(usually named \fIv4linux-PID.v4dump\fR). It reads the container file,
verifies integrity via CRC32, decodes machine state at crash time, and
presents memory mappings, process metadata, and execution state.
.PP
When invoked without selection options, a summary is displayed showing
dump identity, crash details (signal, faulting address, executing instruction),
general-purpose registers, condition codes, and memory layout.
.PP
The following options are available:
.TP
.B \-a
Display all standard information sections: crash analysis, registers,
floating point status, process details, backtrace, container section
headers, and 16 words of stack. Excludes full memory dumps and the
complete symbol table.
.TP
.B \-r
Print the general-purpose registers (\fBr0\fR\(en\fBr5\fR, \fBsp\fR,
\fBpc\fR) and the processor status word (\fBps\fR) condition code flags
(\fBN\fR, \fBZ\fR, \fBV\fR, \fBC\fR).
.TP
.B \-f
Display floating-point unit (FPU) status, condition codes, and floating
point accumulators (\fBac0\fR\(en\fBac5\fR).
.TP
.B \-l
Display the virtual memory layout (text, data, bss, break/heap, and stack
ranges and utilization), mapped regions, and container file sections.
.TP
.B \-p
Display process context: guest PID and PPID, host process ID, executable
path, root directory, current working directory, command-line arguments
(\fBargv\fR), signal handlers, pending signals, and open guest-to-host
file descriptor mappings.
.TP
.B \-b
Print the call backtrace recorded at the time of the crash.
.TP
.B \-y
Print the program symbol table sorted by address and name. Output includes
the symbol value, type letter (\fBU\fR, \fBA\fR, \fBT\fR, \fBD\fR, or \fBB\fR;
lowercase if local), and symbol name.
.TP
.B \-s \fIwords\fR
Print \fIwords\fR 16-bit words of stack memory starting at the current
stack pointer (\fBsp\fR), resolving addresses to nearest symbols where
possible and annotating register pointers.
.TP
.B \-m \fIaddr\fR[\fB,\fIlen\fR]
Print an octal and ASCII memory dump starting at byte address \fIaddr\fR
for \fIlen\fR bytes (default is 0200 bytes). Number arguments follow C
syntax; addresses prefixed with \fB0\fR are interpreted as octal.
.TP
.B \-c
Check mode. Verifies the file size and CRC32 checksum of \fIdumpfile\fR
without printing dump contents.
.TP
.BI \-x\  format
Extract the program image from the dump into the file specified by
\fB\-o\fR. Supported formats are:
.RS
.TP
.B aout
Reconstructs a PDP-11 \fBa.out\fR executable (\fB0407\fR impure or
\fB0410\fR shared text) containing the memory contents at crash time and
restoring symbol tables for analysis with \fBv4-disasm\fR(1).
.TP
.B raw
Extracts the full, flat 64KB PDP-11 memory space.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-o\  file
Specify the destination file for image extraction with \fB\-x\fR.
.TP
.B \-h
Display a summary of command-line options and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I v4linux-PID.v4dump
Standard filename pattern for \fBv4linux\fR crash dump container files.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or verified integrity in check mode (\fB\-c\fR).
.TP
.B 1
CRC mismatch, file corruption, or read/write error.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line syntax or argument error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR "v4-disasm\fR" (1),
.BR "v4-as"(1),
.BR "v4dump" (5)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
If the file does not begin with the expected container magic, the message
\fI"not a V4DUMP crash dump (bad magic)"\fR is printed.
.PP
If the header version does not match the toolchain version, the message
\fI"unsupported dump format version"\fR is printed.
.PP
If the recorded file size or CRC32 fails verification, a warning or damage
diagnostic is emitted.
.SH BUGS
Code compiled with non-standard calling
conventions may produce incomplete backtraces.